**Declaration.** `input<Order>()` produces an `InputSignal<Order>`. Since `order` is a required value the component can't render without, use `input.required<Order>()` — it removes `undefined` from the type and makes the compiler flag any call site that forgets to bind it. If a default were meaningful, `input<Order>(defaultOrder)` would be the form instead. Keep the explicit generic (or explicit initial value) so the signal is typed rather than inferred as `unknown`.

**Reading it.** An `InputSignal` is a getter, not a property — you call `order()` in the component class and in the template, you never read `this.order` as a value. That's the single most common mistake coming from decorator inputs.

**Derivation.** Any ReactOn the change-detection side, leave the component on `OnPush`. Because the template reads `order()` (and `total()`) as signals, Angular registers those signals as dependencies of the component's view and re-renders only when one of them changes — you get correct updates without `Default`, and without manually implementing `ngOnChanges`.

**Templates.** The template above is inline, but if the project splits templates into `.html` files, the same rule applies there: `order().id`, not `order.id`. If the surrounding code uses `@if`/`@for`, wrap any optional rendering in `@if (order().lines.length) { ... }` rather than `*ngIf`.

**One caveat on `computed()`.** It has to stay pure — `total()` above only reads `order()`, which is correct. Don't do the fetch-into-a-signal work, HTTP calls, or writes to other signals inside a `computed()`; if you need to react to `order()` changing with a side effect (say, refetching line-item detail), use an `effect()` or the RxJS interop boundary (`toSignal`/`takeUntilDestroyed`) instead.

Consistency matters more than novelty: if you glance at a neighboring component and see it declaring its inputs this way, mirror it exactly — required vs. optional, naming, and where the type is written. A file that mixes `input()` with stray `@Input()` fields is the one thing worth avoiding.

