```

Key points:

**Required vs optional.** `input.required<Order>()` for a value the component cannot function without — Angular enforces this at the type level, so a caller that forgets `[order]` gets a compile error under `strictTemplates`. If the input is genuinely optional, use `input<Order>()` and read it as `Order | undefined`, or give a default via `input(defaultOrder)`.

**It is a read-only signal.** `order()` is the only way to read it. There is no `this.order` field holding the value, so any code that previously did `this.order.id` becomes `this.order().id`. This is the point of the migration, not incidental friction — the template read of `order()` is what registers the dependency and lets `OnPush` work with no extra wiring.

**Use it, don't copy it.** Because it is a signal, `computed()` and `effect()` can depend on it directly, as in `total` above. Do not mirror it into a separate `signal()` field in `ngOnInit` or a setter — that reintroduces the manual-sync bug the signal input exists to remove, and the copy will silently go stale if a parent rebinds while the computed keeps reading the mirror.

**Derive with `computed()`, not a method.** `computed(() => summarize(this.order()))` memoizes on identity of the input; a plain getter or method re-runs on every change-detection pass. Keep the computed pure — no HTTP, no writes to other signals — per the project's patterns.

**Output side.** If this component emits anything back, use `output<OrderChange>()` rather than `@Output() EventEmitter`, to stay consistent with the signal-based style throughout.

**Two traps to avoid**

- Don't call `inject()` or read `this.order()` inside a `setTimeout`/promise callback created before inputs are set and assume it's available — read the signal at the time you need it, after the component is initialized, not in a field initializer ordering you don't control.
- Don't leave `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` "just in case the input doesn't propagate." With `input()` plus a `computed()` read in the template, `OnPush` tracks the real dependency; `Default` only makes the component re-check on every app-wide pass for no benefit.

