No — that will fail at runtime. Calling `inject()` inside the `setTimeout` callback throws an error, because that callback executes later, outside the injection context. It is not a compile-time error, so it may slip through until the timer actually fires.

The injection context only exists during the class construction phase: field initializers and the constructor body. Capture the dependency there and use it via closure inside the callback.

Do this instead:

```ts
export class MyComponent implements OnInit {
  private readonly myService = inject(MyService);

  ngOnInit(): void {
    setTimeout(() => {
      this.myService.doSomething();
    }, 500);
  }
}
```

Key points:

- `inject()` lives in a field initializer, so the injection context is available.
- The `setTimeout` callback closes over `this.myService`, which is already resolved.
- Do not try `inject()` inside `ngOnInit` either — that is outside the injection context as well for a constructor-only capture. Injecting as a field initializer is the correct placement.

When your team needs a dependency that is only known at callback time, use `Injector` instead:

```ts
private readonly injector = inject(Injector);

ngOnInit(): void {
  setTimeout(() => {
    const svc = this.injector.get(MyService);
    svc.doSomething();
  }, 500);
}
```

`Injector.get()` is valid anywhere once you hold an `Injector` reference, so it works inside the timeout. Prefer the direct field-injection form when the dependency is known up front — only reach for `Injector` when the dependency genuinely depends on runtime state.

One more thing while you are in this area: if the service call returns an `Observable`, do not just fire and forget it inside the timeout. Pipe it through `takeUntilDestroyed()` or convert with `toSignal()` so the subscription is torn down when the component is destroyed. An unmanaged subscription from a timer callback outliving the component is a real leak.

